<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andean civilizations</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Andean civilizations were complex societies of many indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, stretching 4,000 km along the Andes from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> to north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and northwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a> or Norte Chico civilisation of coastal Peru, dating back to 3500 BCE, is the oldest known civilisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>; later came Chavín, Paracas, Nazca, Moche, Wari, Tiwanaku and Chimor. They had no writing, wheeled vehicles or money, recorded information on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quipu">quipu</a> and domesticated crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, quinoa and coca. From 1438 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> united most of them in one empire, before the Spanish conquest of the 16th century.</p></div>
